/-!
# The completed stalk of a coherent adic system is a finite module

Let `X` be a locally Noetherian scheme, let `ℐ` be a quasi-coherent sheaf of ideals on `X` and let
`(ℱ_n)` be an object of `Coh(X, ℐ)`. At a point `x` the formal stalk is the inverse limit
`ℱ_x^∧ = lim ℱ_{n,x}` of the stalks, and this file proves that it is a **finite module over the
completed local ring** `𝒪_{X,x}^∧`.

The route is the local analogue of the affine statement
`Stacks.Coherent.finite_globalSectionsLimit_of_isAdicComplete`. Each `ℱ_n` is coherent, so
`ℱ_{n,x}` is a finite `𝒪_{X,x}`-module; the transition `ℱ_{n+1} → ℱ_n` is the projection onto the
quotient by `ℐ^{n+1} ℱ_{n+1}`, and the stalk functor is exact, so on stalks it is surjective with
kernel `ℐ_x^{n+1} ℱ_{n+1,x}`. The tower machinery of Stacks 09B8 then identifies the reduction
`ℱ_x^∧ / ℐ_x ℱ_x^∧` with `ℱ_{0,x}`, which is finite, and Stacks 031D upgrades a module with finite
reduction to one whose completion is finite over the completed ring.

Nothing in the level-zero reduction is special to level zero: the limit projections are
surjective with kernel the `(n+1)`-st ideal-power multiple at every level, and that is all the
identification uses.

The computation of the kernel is the one geometric input that is not formal: it needs the stalk of
the subsheaf `ℐ^n ℳ` to be `ℐ_x^n ℳ_x`, which is proved here from the affine sections
`Γ(ℐ^n ℳ, U) = ℐ(U)^n Γ(ℳ, U)` of a quasi-coherent `ℳ` together with the description of `ℐ_x` as
the ideal generated by the germs of the sections of `ℐ` on an affine neighbourhood.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Algebraization.stalk_idealPowerMultiple` — the stalk of `ℐ^n ℳ` inside `ℳ_x` is
  `ℐ_x^n ℳ_x`, for `ℳ` quasi-coherent.
* `Stacks.Algebraization.surjective_stalk_coherentAdicTransition` and
  `Stacks.Algebraization.ker_stalk_coherentAdicTransition` — on stalks, a transition map of a
  coherent adic system is surjective with kernel the corresponding ideal-power multiple.
* `Stacks.Algebraization.finite_quotient_formalStalk` — the reduction `ℱ_x^∧ / ℐ_x ℱ_x^∧` is a
  finite `𝒪_{X,x}`-module.
* `Stacks.Algebraization.formalStalkQuotientEquivStalk` and
  `Stacks.Algebraization.completedStalkQuotientEquivStalk` — the reduction at **every** level:
  `ℱ_x^∧ / ℐ_x^{n+1} ℱ_x^∧ ≅ ℱ_{n,x}`, the second with the quotient taken by a submodule over the
  completed local ring, which is the ring that acts on `ℱ_x^∧`.
* `Stacks.Algebraization.finite_completedStalk` — **the completed stalk is a finite module over
  the completed local ring**, with
  `Stacks.Algebraization.finite_completedStalk_of_isNoetherian` the same statement for a
  Noetherian scheme.

## Implementation notes

`Stacks.Algebraization.formalStalk` is a limit of `Stacks.Algebraization.stalkModuleFunctor`, the
stalk functor written with `RingCat`-valued scalars restricted along
`SemilinearStalk.commStalkIso`. All the germ computations and the exactness instances available
for the stalk of a sheaf of modules on a scheme are stated instead for
`Stacks.ModuleSkyscraper.modulesStalkFunctor`, whose values carry Mathlib's own
`𝒪_{X,x}`-action on the stalk of the underlying presheaf. The two functors are isomorphic by
`AlgebraicGeometry.restrictScalarsStalkFunctorIso`, whose restriction-of-scalars ring map is the
same as `stalkModuleFunctor`'s; everything below is proved in the `modulesStalkFunctor` spelling
and transported once, at `finite_quotient_formalStalk`.

The ideal-power multiple of a coherent adic system is
`Stacks.Algebraization.idealPowerMultiple`, an infimum of subsheaves, so its sections are not
given by the definition. `Stacks.Algebraization.idealPowerMultiple_toSubmodule_obj_affine`
supplies them on an affine open, and that is the only place quasi-coherence is used.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0EJE](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0EJE)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0EJ3](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0EJ3)
